      "dom_id": "question-31979",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Factor $9{x}^{2}-12x+4$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-12x+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-12x+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(6x)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $3x$ and $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3x$ and $b=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
