      "name": null,
      "problem": "Factor $-9{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{4}{9}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{4}{9} & = \\\\ -(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-4x+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3x)+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9}) & = \\\\ -{(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
